{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, none of these; the sum is 3/16","reason":"Each pair is 9/49 times a successive power of 1/49. The sum is (9/49)/(1−1/49)=9/48=3/16, which is not listed.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1950_AMC_12_Q43","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h207"],"id":"pair-terms","label":"Group consecutive terms","methodDirectNodeIds":{"pair-terms":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h207"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, a^2/(1-r^2)","reason":"The squared terms are a^2,a^2r^2,a^2r^4,... . Since |r|<1 implies r^2<1, their sum is a^2/(1-r^2).","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1951_AMC_12_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_143"],"id":"1951_AMC_12_Q11_m1","label":"Identify the series of squares","methodDirectNodeIds":{"1951_AMC_12_Q11_m1":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_143"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 9 or 3","reason":"Let the first term be a and ratio r. Then a/(1-r)=6 and a(1+r)=4.5. Thus 6(1-r²)=4.5, so r=±1/2. Both satisfy |r|<1, and a=6(1-r) is 3 or 9.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1952_AMC_12_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h207","cnm_075"],"id":"scaled-tail","label":"Compare the tail with the whole series","methodDirectNodeIds":{"scaled-tail":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_220"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_220","cnm_h207","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 4π/3 square inches","reason":"The areas are π, π/4, π/16, and so on. Since the ratio is 1/4<1, their sum is π/(1-1/4)=4π/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1953_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p192"],"id":"sum-geometric-areas","label":"Find the geometric series of areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-geometric-areas":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_p192"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 2.4","reason":"The first term is 4 and the ratio is -2/3, whose absolute value is below 1. Thus s=4/(1+2/3)=12/5=2.4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1962_AMC_12_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h207"],"id":"geometric-sum","label":"Use the infinite geometric sum","methodDirectNodeIds":{"geometric-sum":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h207"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 3/5","reason":"Convergence gives 1/(1−cos²θ) = 5, hence cos²θ = 4/5. Therefore cos 2θ = 2(4/5)−1 = 3/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_12A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"geometric-series-double-angle","label":"Find the common ratio","methodDirectNodeIds":{"geometric-series-double-angle":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h073"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h073"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 1","reason":"Positivity and finite sums imply 0<r_j<1. Each ratio satisfies r_j²-r_j+a=0. Since the series are different, r_1 and r_2 are its two distinct roots, whose sum is 1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2009_AMC_12A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h207","cnm_147","cnm_150","cnm_040"],"id":"solution:subtract-and-factor:7f03d9653894","label":"Eliminate the common first term","methodDirectNodeIds":{"subtract-and-factor":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_152","cnm_151"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_152","cnm_151","cnm_h207","cnm_147","cnm_150","cnm_040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E (4)","reason":"If the first term is a and the ratio is r, then ar=1 and S=a/(1-r)=1/[r(1-r)]. Convergence and S>0 force 0<r<1. Since r(1-r)=1/4-(r-1/2)^2≤1/4, S≥4. Equality occurs for r=1/2 and a=2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_10B_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h207","cnm_148","cnm_096"],"id":"maximize-positive-denominator","label":"Maximize the denominator","methodDirectNodeIds":{"maximize-positive-denominator":["math:infinite-geometric-series","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_235"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_235","cnm_h207","cnm_148","cnm_096"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E (4)","reason":"If the first term is a, then ar=1 and S=a/(1-r)=1/[r(1-r)]. Convergence and S>0 imply 0<r<1. Since r(1-r)≤1/4, S≥4, attained at r=1/2 and a=2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_12B_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h207","cnm_148"],"id":"solution:quadratic-denominator:60fc96dfc60b","label":"Maximize the positive denominator","methodDirectNodeIds":{"quadratic-denominator":["math:infinite-geometric-series","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_235"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_235","cnm_h207","cnm_148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 19, with sum 15/4","reason":"The sum is (sum 2^(-i))(sum 3^(-j))(sum 5^(-k)), with all indices nonnegative. Each ratio has absolute value less than 1, so the product is 2 times (3/2) times (5/4) = 15/4. Unique factorization prevents repetition, and positive terms justify the product expansion. Thus m + n = 19.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12A_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"product-of-geometric-series","label":"Multiply three geometric series","methodDirectNodeIds":{"product-of-geometric-series":["math:prime-factorization","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","math:infinite-geometric-series"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: (4+√2)/3 inches","reason":"The rational terms sum to 1/(1-1/4)=4/3, and the radical terms sum to (√2/4)/(1-1/4)=√2/3. Their sum is (4+√2)/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1952_AMC_12_Q50","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h207"],"id":"paired-geometric-series","label":"Group consecutive terms into geometric pairs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"paired-geometric-series":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h210"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h210","cnm_h207"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 2/7","reason":"The series converges absolutely because its absolute terms form a geometric series with ratio 1/2. Each three-term block is (1/4)(1/8)^k, so S=(1/4)/(1-1/8)=2/7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1959_AMC_12_Q49","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h207","cnm_h210"],"id":"three-term-blocks","label":"Group consecutive triples","methodDirectNodeIds":{"three-term-blocks":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h207","cnm_h210"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E (5/2)","reason":"The even-power terms sum to 7-3=4. The odd-power sum is r times this, so r=3/4. Then a=4(1-r²)=7/4, giving a+r=5/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_12B_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h207","cnm_p145"],"id":"compare-even-and-odd-sums","label":"Compare the even- and odd-power sums","methodDirectNodeIds":{"compare-even-and-odd-sums":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h207","cnm_p145"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 1/3","reason":"For k>=1, floor(log2(x))=-k on [2^(-k),2^(1-k)), with the original open-domain endpoint convention. Its length is 2^(-k); independence gives matching-bin square area 4^(-k). Summing gives (1/4)/(1-1/4)=1/3, D. Alternatively both upper-half draws contribute 1/4, both lower-half draws contribute P/4 after rescaling, and mixed halves fail, so P=1/4+P/4. Boundary points have zero area. Reuse exact geometric probability and floor/logarithm support while preserving the infinite-series and recursive alternatives as OR routes, never universal requirements.","recordedAnswer":"D","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_12B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"dyadic-interval-sum","label":"Sum equal dyadic-bin areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"dyadic-interval-sum":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"matching-square-areas","label":"Sum matching square areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"matching-square-areas":["math:floor-ceiling","math:geometric-probability","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h047"]},"nodeIds":["math:floor-ceiling","math:geometric-probability","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h047"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 6/5 kilometers","reason":"Let u_n be Henry's distance from home after 2n turns. Then u_0=0 and u_(n+1)=u_n/16+3/8, so u_n=(2/5)(1-16^(-n)) tends to 2/5. The other turnaround position tends to 3/2+(2/5)/4=8/5. Their separation is 6/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_10B_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"two-walk-recurrence","label":"Combine pairs of walks","methodDirectNodeIds":{"two-walk-recurrence":["cnm_h200","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_p179"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h200","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_p179"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 1/24","reason":"The eligible stopped strings are T(HT)^jHH for j≥1, with lengths 2j+3. Their probabilities sum to 1/32+1/128+...=(1/32)/(1-1/4)=1/24.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_10B_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"alternating-history-sum","label":"Sum alternating winning histories","methodDirectNodeIds":{"alternating-history-sum":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h138","cnm_h141"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h138","cnm_h141"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (sqrt(10))","reason":"The first N factors have product 10^((1-3^(-N))/2). As N tends to infinity, the exponent tends to 1/2, giving sqrt(10).","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12A_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"sum-geometric-exponents","label":"Add the exponents","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-geometric-exponents":["cnm_h039","cnm_h040","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h039","cnm_h040","math:infinite-geometric-series"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"adjudication":"Root independently inspected the displayed stem, choices and mathematical counterexamples or ordinary task convention.","derivedAnswer":"Statements 3, 4 and 5 under the usual limiting reading; E under a literal fixed-term reading","reason":"The terms a_n=2*(1/2)^(n-1) tend to zero and partial sums S_n=4*(1-2^-n) tend to 4. Thus statements 3,4,5 hold if the index may be chosen sufficiently large, but that combination is absent. Reading any term as a fixed previously chosen term makes statement3 false and explains E. The wording leaves a genuine quantifier ambiguity; preserve both readings rather than penalizing the usual sequence-limit interpretation.","recordedAnswer":"E","status":"confirmed_ambiguous"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["No answer option lists all three true statements.","Statement (3) is read in its usual limiting sense, with the term index increasing.","No answer option lists all three statements (3), (4), and (5).","Statement (3) uses imprecise wording. If 'any term' means an arbitrary fixed term rather than a sufficiently late term, it is false and option E results. Clarification is needed before assigning a unique listed choice.","confirmed_answer_ambiguity"],"questionId":"1950_AMC_12_Q39","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h207"],"id":"terms-and-partial-sums","label":"Compare the terms with the partial-sum error","methodDirectNodeIds":{"terms-and-partial-sums":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h208","cnm_h210"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h208","cnm_h210","cnm_h207"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 5","reason":"Let the first term be a. Then a/(1-r)=15 and a^2/(1-r^2)=45. Dividing 225 by 45 gives (1+r)/(1-r)=5, so r=2/3 and a=15(1-r)=5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1970_AMC_12_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_147","cnm_160","cnm_042"],"id":"geometric-sums","label":"Relate the two geometric sums","methodDirectNodeIds":{"geometric-sums":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_147","cnm_160","cnm_042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 8/3","reason":"Write the first term as a positive integer a and the ratio as 1/m. Convergence requires the integer m≥2. From a/(1-1/m)=3, we obtain a=3-3/m, so m divides 3. Thus m=3, a=2, and the first two terms sum to 2+2/3=8/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1975_AMC_12_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h207","cnm_p153"],"id":"geometric-sum-and-integrality","label":"Use the sum and the integer restrictions","methodDirectNodeIds":{"geometric-sum-and-integrality":["math:infinite-geometric-series","math:integer-diophantine-equations"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_h207","cnm_p153"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 1/3","reason":"P(R=G)=sum from k=1 to infinity of 2^(-2k)=(1/4)/(1-1/4)=1/3. Since P(R>G)=P(G>R), the requested probability is (1-1/3)/2=1/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_10B_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"ties-and-symmetry","label":"Sum ties and use symmetry","methodDirectNodeIds":{"ties-and-symmetry":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h139","cnm_h140","cnm_h138"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h139","cnm_h140","cnm_h138"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (1/3)","reason":"The tie probability is sum from k=1 to infinity of 2^(-k)·2^(-k) = (1/4)/(1-1/4) = 1/3. The two strict orderings have equal probability, so P(red > green) = (1-1/3)/2 = 1/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_12B_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"ties-and-color-symmetry","label":"Find ties, then use color symmetry","methodDirectNodeIds":{"ties-and-color-symmetry":["cnm_h140","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h138","cnm_h139"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h140","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h138","cnm_h139"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 7/16","reason":"Squaring a_n+i*b_n gives (3+4*i)^n, whose imaginary part is 2*a_n*b_n. Put z=(3+4*i)/7, with modulus 5/7. For every finite N, direct multiplication gives (1-z)*(1+z+...+z^N)=1-z^(N+1). Thus the requested real partial sum is half the imaginary part of (1-z^(N+1))/(1-z). Its remainder has absolute value at most (1/2)*(5/7)^(N+1)/|1-z|, tending to zero by real geometric decay. Finally 1/(1-z)=7/(4-4*i)=7/8+(7/8)*i, yielding 7/16, B. Reuse this complete finite-complex telescope and real remainder route. The real-ratio infinite-series node is used only for its real majorant; its definition is not widened to complex ratios.","recordedAnswer":"B","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_12A_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"finite-complex-telescope-real-remainder","label":"Telescope finite complex powers and bound the remainder by a real geometric sequence","methodDirectNodeIds":{"finite-complex-telescope-real-remainder":["cnm_h102","cnm_h103","cnm_h101","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h102","cnm_h103","cnm_h101","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"finite-complex-geometric-sum","label":"Square and sum finite powers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"finite-complex-geometric-sum":["cnm_h102","cnm_h103","cnm_h098","cnm_h210","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h102","cnm_h103","cnm_h098","cnm_h210","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"sum-real-recurrences","label":"Sum two real recurrences","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-real-recurrences":["cnm_h102","cnm_h200","cnm_089","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h102","cnm_h200","cnm_089","math:infinite-geometric-series"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: (4/5,2/5)","reason":"The horizontal coordinate tends to 1-1/4+1/16-⋯=1/(1+1/4)=4/5. The vertical coordinate tends to 1/2-1/8+1/32-⋯=(1/2)/(1+1/4)=2/5. The bug approaches this point arbitrarily closely.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1980_AMC_12_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h207","cnm_082","cnm_010"],"id":"coordinate-series","label":"Sum the horizontal and vertical movements","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-series":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_086"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_086","cnm_h207","cnm_082","cnm_010"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. 55","reason":"For fixed a,d≥1, the six assignments to bins a,a+d,a+2d have total probability 6·2^(-3a-3d). Thus P=6(Σ from a=1 to ∞ of 8^(-a))(Σ from d=1 to ∞ of 8^(-d))=6(1/7)²=6/49. Hence p+q=55.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_12B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"sum-over-start-and-spacing","label":"Choose the first bin and the spacing","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-over-start-and-spacing":["cnm_h140","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h202","cnm_h138","cnm_h234"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h140","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h202","cnm_h138","cnm_h234"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"shift-invariance-recurrence","label":"Shift the bin numbers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"shift-invariance-recurrence":["math:recursive-counting-probability","cnm_h140","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h202","cnm_h138","cnm_h234"]},"nodeIds":["math:recursive-counting-probability","cnm_h140","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h202","cnm_h138","cnm_h234"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 6a","reason":"The perimeters are 3a, 3a/2, 3a/4, and so on. Since the common ratio is 1/2, their sum is 3a/(1-1/2)=6a.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1951_AMC_12_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_p122","cnm_040"],"id":"1951_AMC_12_Q09_m1","label":"Sum the successively halved perimeters","methodDirectNodeIds":{"1951_AMC_12_Q09_m1":["cnm_185","cnm_h210","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_185","cnm_h210","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_055","cnm_p122","cnm_040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, πm²/2","reason":"The first circle has area πm²/4. Each new square has half the preceding square's area, so consecutive circle areas have ratio 1/2. Their sum is (πm²/4)/(1-1/2)=πm²/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1967_AMC_12_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_191","cnm_174","cnm_p188","cnm_p192"],"id":"circle-area-geometric-sum","label":"Find the area ratio and sum","methodDirectNodeIds":{"circle-area-geometric-sum":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h210"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h210","cnm_191","cnm_174","cnm_p188","cnm_p192"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, a^2/(a-b)","reason":"The common ratio is q=b/a, equal to the absolute cosine of the angle between the distinct lines, so 0≤q<1. The sum is a/(1-q)=a^2/(a-b).","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["The displayed formula assumes the initial point is not O, so a>0. Starting at O gives the degenerate zero-length sequence.","The listed formula assumes the initial point is not O, so a>0. Starting at O gives an all-zero sequence, whose sum is 0, while the listed expression becomes undefined."],"questionId":"1965_AMC_12_Q36","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_064","cnm_270","cnm_040"],"id":"similar-triangles-geometric-sum","label":"Find the repeated scale factor","methodDirectNodeIds":{"similar-triangles-geometric-sum":["cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_h210","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_h210","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_064","cnm_270","cnm_040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: (16+12*sqrt(2)):1","reason":"Centers of circles of radii R and r are (R,R) and (r,r), so sqrt(2)(R-r) = R+r. Hence q = r/R = 3-2*sqrt(2). The requested ratio is (1-q^2)/q^2 = 16+12*sqrt(2).","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1971_AMC_12_Q35","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_262","cnm_174","cnm_p192","cnm_173"],"id":"tangency-and-geometric-series","label":"Find the radius ratio, then sum the areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangency-and-geometric-series":["cnm_h181","cnm_h207","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h181","cnm_h207","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_082","cnm_262","cnm_174","cnm_p192","cnm_173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":0,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: approximately 4/7","reason":"Since the common ratio is 1/8<1, Sarika's limiting share is (1/2)/(1-1/8)=4/7, approximating her share when the remaining cheese is negligible.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["The visibility cutoff is unspecified, so 4/7 is the limiting share rather than an exact finite-stop fraction."],"questionId":"2025_AMC_8_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"geometric-series-sum","label":"Sum Sarika's servings","methodDirectNodeIds":{"geometric-series-sum":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 349","reason":"For u=2.525252…, subtracting u from 100u gives 99u=250. Thus u=250/99 in lowest terms, and 250+99=349.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1960_AMC_12_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"sum-geometric-series","label":"Sum the convergent repeating tail","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-geometric-series":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 2/3","reason":"Two misses return the game to its initial state with probability 1/4. Thus Larry's win probability p satisfies p = 1/2 + p/4, giving p = 2/3. Equivalently, his winning probabilities sum to (1/2)/(1-1/4).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2015_AMC_12B_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"geometric-winning-turns","label":"Sum Larry's winning turns","methodDirectNodeIds":{"geometric-winning-turns":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 75","reason":"The repeating number is 1 + n/99, while the truncated number is 1 + n/100. Their products differ by 66n(1/99 - 1/100) = n/150 = 0.5, giving n = 75.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_10A_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"sum-geometric-decimal-tail","label":"Sum the repeating geometric tail","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-geometric-decimal-tail":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D (13)","reason":"The equation becomes (100a+10b+c)/999=(a+b+c)/27, hence 7a=3b+4c. Modulo 7 this gives b≡c, so b-c is 0, 7, or -7. These cases give 9, 2, and 2 valid digit triples, respectively, for a total of 13.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_10A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"geometric-series-conversion","label":"Sum convergent geometric series","methodDirectNodeIds":{"geometric-series-conversion":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. 15","reason":"If t=0.363636..., then 100t-t=36, so t=36/99=4/11. The numerator and denominator sum to 4+11=15.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1965_AMC_12_Q05","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"geometric-series","label":"Sum the repeating place-value blocks","methodDirectNodeIds":{"geometric-series":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. 57","reason":"1000F-10F=477, so F=477/990=53/110. The denominator exceeds the numerator by 110-53=57.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1970_AMC_12_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"sum-repeating-tail","label":"Sum the repeating geometric tail","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-repeating-tail":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 25/91","reason":"Writing p for Carol's probability gives p=25/216+(125/216)p, since a completely unsuccessful round resets the situation. Thus p=25/91. The same result follows by summing the geometric series with ratio 125/216.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1981_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"geometric-probability-series","label":"Sum over completed rounds","methodDirectNodeIds":{"geometric-probability-series":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h138"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h138"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h140","cnm_h207"],"id":"sum-winning-tosses","label":"Sum over Carol's possible winning tosses","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-winning-tosses":["cnm_h141","cnm_h138","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h141","cnm_h138","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h140","cnm_h207"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D (143)","reason":"Since 1/n=(10a+b)/99, n must divide 99. The distinct-digit blocks 09, 03, and 01 give n=11, 33, and 99. The other divisors give equal-digit blocks, so the required sum is 11+33+99=143.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_12A_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"sum-repeating-blocks","label":"Sum a geometric series","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-repeating-blocks":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (1/7)","reason":"One player's first head occurs on flip n with probability 2^(-n). All three stop on that flip with probability 8^(-n), so the required probability is Σ(n≥1)8^(-n)=(1/8)/(1-1/8)=1/7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_12B_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"sum-common-stopping-times","label":"Sum over common stopping times","nodeIds":["cnm_h138","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h140","cnm_h138"],"id":"solution:sum-common-stopping-times:58e1e6751923","label":"Sum over the common stopping flip","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-common-stopping-times":["cnm_h141","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h141","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h140","cnm_h138"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 16","reason":"The repeating value is (2k+3)/(k²−1), so 7(k²−1)=51(2k+3). This gives 7k²−102k−160=(k−16)(7k+10)=0. Only k=16 is an admissible base, and 35/255=7/51 verifies it.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_10A_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"sum-blocks-and-solve","label":"Geometric series and quadratic formula","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-blocks-and-solve":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_222"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_222"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: k = 16","reason":"The repeating expansion equals (2k+3)/(k²-1). Equating this to 7/51 gives 7k²-102k-160 = (k-16)(7k+10) = 0. The only valid integer base greater than 3 is 16.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_12A_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"sum-repeating-blocks","label":"Sum a geometric series","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-repeating-blocks":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_224"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 75","reason":"The two multipliers are 1+N/99 and 1+N/100. Their product difference is 66N(1/99-1/100) = N/150 = 0.5, so N = 75.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_12A_Q05","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"geometric-series-conversion","label":"Sum the repeating decimal as a series","methodDirectNodeIds":{"geometric-series-conversion":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 3/4","reason":"Normalize the outer area to 1. The shaded area is (1-k²)∑_{j≥0}k^(4j)=1/(1+k²). Setting this equal to 16/25 gives k²=9/16, and 0<k<1 selects k=3/4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_10A_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"shaded-ring-series","label":"Sum the shaded rings","methodDirectNodeIds":{"shaded-ring-series":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_220"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_220"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 1/97","reason":"Let p_i be the probability of reaching 4 from i. Then p_4=1, p_0=p_1/2, and 4p_i=p_(i-1)+p_(i+1) for i=1,2,3. Successive substitution gives p_1=2p_0, p_2=7p_0, p_3=26p_0, and p_4=97p_0. Hence p_0=1/97. Survival beyond m moves has probability at most 2^(-m), so indefinite survival contributes no additional probability.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_10B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"sum-return-excursions","label":"Sum repeated return excursions","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-return-excursions":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h245"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h245"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"successive-crossings-and-return-excursions","label":"Cross one level at a time","methodDirectNodeIds":{"successive-crossings-and-return-excursions":["math:recursive-counting-probability","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h245"]},"nodeIds":["math:recursive-counting-probability","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h245"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, k=3/4","reason":"Normalize the outside area to 1. Since 0<k<1, the shaded area is (1-k²)(1+k⁴+k⁸+...)=1/(1+k²). Setting this equal to 16/25 gives k²=9/16, hence k=3/4. Equivalently, the unshaded area equals k² times the shaded area.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_12A_Q05","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"alternating-geometric-series","label":"Sum the shaded square frames","methodDirectNodeIds":{"alternating-geometric-series":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_220"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_220"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 1/97","reason":"Let p_j be the probability of reaching 4 from j. Then p_0=p_1/2, 4p_j=p_{j-1}+p_{j+1} for j=1,2,3, and p_4=1. Successive substitution gives p_1=2p_0, p_2=7p_0, p_3=26p_0, and p_4=97p_0. Hence p_0=1/97.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_12B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"successive-crossings-and-return-excursions","label":"Cross one level at a time","methodDirectNodeIds":{"successive-crossings-and-return-excursions":["math:recursive-counting-probability","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h245"]},"nodeIds":["math:recursive-counting-probability","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h245"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 10/81","reason":"For S_N=sum from n=1 to N of n/10^n, shift-subtraction gives (9/10)S_N=(1/9)(1-10^-N)-N/10^(N+1). Both remainder terms tend to zero, so S=10/81.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1962_AMC_12_Q40","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h207","cnm_018"],"id":"shift-subtract-partial-sums","label":"Shift and subtract finite partial sums","methodDirectNodeIds":{"shift-subtract-partial-sums":["math:telescoping","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:telescoping","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h207","cnm_018"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 19","reason":"Writing u=R1 and v=R2, the fractions are (3u+7)/(u²-1) and (7u+3)/(u²-1), with analogous numerators 2v+5 and 5v+2. Adding and subtracting the two equalities gives 10v-7u=3 and 4v-3u=-1. Hence u=11, v=8, and their sum is 19.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1966_AMC_12_Q39","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"geometric-place-values","label":"Sum repeating place-value series","methodDirectNodeIds":{"geometric-place-values":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_161","cnm_090"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_161","cnm_090"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 5 denominators","reason":"Let k=10a+b. The reduced denominator is 99/gcd(k,99). Since 99=3^2·11 and k is neither 0 nor 99, the possible denominators are precisely 3, 9, 11, 33, and 99. Each is attained by taking k=99/d.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2002_AMC_12A_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"repeating-geometric-series","label":"Sum the repeating blocks","methodDirectNodeIds":{"repeating-geometric-series":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 1/2","reason":"Each path ending at 10 is determined by a subset of the nine intermediate integers, giving 2^9 paths. Every such path has jump lengths summing to 10, so its probability is 2^-10. Adding these disjoint probabilities gives 2^9·2^-10=1/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_12A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"balance-hit-and-overshoot","label":"Balance landing and overshooting","methodDirectNodeIds":{"balance-hit-and-overshoot":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h140","cnm_h138","cnm_h139"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h140","cnm_h138","cnm_h139"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 7/2","reason":"For n≥1, P(T>n)=(1/3)^n+(2/3)^n. These geometric tails are summable, establishing finiteness, and E[T]=1+Σ_{n≥1}P(T>n)=1+1/2+2=7/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_12B_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"sum-survival-probabilities","label":"Sum survival probabilities","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-survival-probabilities":["cnm_h252","cnm_h141","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h252","cnm_h141"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 1/6","reason":"In decade [10^-k,10^(1-k)), both floors equal -k exactly for x in [10^-k,(5/2)*10^-k). Sum the disjoint favorable lengths (3/2)*10^-k for k>=1 to obtain 1/6. Decade endpoints do not affect uniform length probability.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["The mathematical value is determined under the customary uniform interpretation of 'chosen at random.'"],"questionId":"2006_AMC_12B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"sum-favorable-decade-lengths","label":"Translate floor values into logarithmic intervals and sum their lengths","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-favorable-decade-lengths":["math:floor-ceiling","cnm_h046","cnm_h047","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:floor-ceiling","cnm_h046","cnm_h047","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_h047","cnm_164"],"id":"sum-decade-lengths","label":"Add the favorable lengths in successive decades","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-decade-lengths":["math:geometric-probability","math:floor-ceiling","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["math:geometric-probability","math:floor-ceiling","math:infinite-geometric-series","cnm_h043","cnm_h047","cnm_164"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 4","reason":"Both players get tails in a round with probability (2/3)(3/5)=2/5. Amelia's winning probability is (1/3)Σₖ₌₀∞(2/5)ᵏ = (1/3)/(1-2/5)=5/9. The series converges since 2/5<1, and q-p=9-5=4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_10A_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"sum-winning-rounds","label":"Sum a geometric series","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-winning-rounds":["cnm_h141","cnm_h138","math:infinite-geometric-series"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h141","cnm_h138","math:infinite-geometric-series"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 962","reason":"Let F(k)=k²+k+1. Then a_k=F(k+1)/((k−1)F(k)), so the product is 2F(100)/(F(4)·98!)=962/98!. A denominator of 97! would require 962/98 to be integral, which it is not; smaller factorials are therefore impossible.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_12A_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:factorial-notation","cnm_p153","cnm_160"],"id":"base-expansion-telescoping","label":"Convert the repeating blocks and telescope","methodDirectNodeIds":{"base-expansion-telescoping":["cnm_024","math:infinite-geometric-series","math:sum-difference-cubes","math:telescoping"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_024","math:infinite-geometric-series","math:sum-difference-cubes","math:telescoping","math:factorial-notation","cnm_p153","cnm_160"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept practice; no claim of complete source-standard coverage or learner mastery.","nextOffset":null,"nodeId":"math:infinite-geometric-series","status":"mapped","total":54}
